2. Getting Started
2.1 System Access and Roles
Access to the Voucher Posting & Authorization System is strictly governed by Role-Based Access Control (RBAC). Your assigned role determines which screens and actions you can perform.
A. Maker Roles (e.g., TCS GLIF & SBI GLIF)
These roles act primarily as the "Makers" (and occasionally intermediate Checkers) of the financial pipeline.
￼ Voucher Posting: Initiate new batches using assigned VIDs (Supports both Manual Entry and Bulk Excel Upload).
￼ Voucher Posting Status: Track the lifecycle, workflow progress, and audit history of submitted batches.
￼ Voucher Authorization: Review, approve, or reject pending batches submitted by other makers.
￼ Inquiry Screens: Access specific screens to search and validate Category-Wise Vouchers, General Ledgers (CGL), Branch Codes, and Asset/Liability configurations.
B. Admin & Final Approver Roles (e.g., SBI GLIF)
These roles act as system administrators, higher-level Checkers, and the "Final Approver" of the system.
￼ Request Voucher: Create and configure the Voucher IDs (VIDs), defining descriptions, issue categories, and target roles.
￼ View Request Voucher: Audit, manage, and terminate existing VID configurations.
￼ Voucher Authorization: Perform the final sign-off on batches and dictate the final Execution Time (Immediate or Scheduled).
￼ Inquiry Screens: Full access to search and validate all System Master data (CGL, Branches, etc.).
2.2 Important Business Rules & Constraints
To maintain the highest level of financial data integrity, the system enforces several non-bypassable constraints. Please keep the following rules in mind:
1. Strict Balancing Rule: For every batch submitted, the Total Debits must perfectly equal the Total Credits. The system enforces this balance at a granular level: batches must balance mathematically per Branch and per Currency.
2. Self-Approval Block (Segregation of Duties): A user is physically restricted from authorizing (Approving or Rejecting) any batch that they created. If a Maker navigates to the Authorization screen, the action buttons for their own batches will be disabled with a security warning.
3. Amount Limitations: Financial amounts cannot be zero. Input fields enforce a strict limit of up to 16 integer digits and exactly 4 decimal places. Negative values are blocked; corrections must be handled via opposite debit/credit entries.
4. Remarks Constraints: Action remarks and row-level remarks are strictly mandatory. They must be between 2 and 30 characters long. To prevent data corruption, the system only accepts alphanumeric characters and single spaces between words.
3. Voucher ID (VID) Creation
3.1 Understanding the Role of the VID
What it is: The VID (Voucher ID) acts as a mandatory authorization ticket that must be generated before any actual financial entries can be posted into the system. It is uniquely generated in the format ⁠VID-YYMM-XXXXX⁠.
Why we use it: Instead of users blindly posting generic vouchers, the VID ensures every posting is tied to a specific, pre-approved business request. It provides lifecycle control—allowing administrators to view all active requests, track which financial batches were posted against them, and dynamically terminate a VID to instantly prevent any further unauthorized postings.
3.2 Creating a New VID
This is the execution step where an administrator submits a structured form to generate the unique identifier. Alongside internal details, the creator can capture an optional SD Request Number (a numeric reference to an external SCORE system request).
Upon clicking "Create," the system validates the inputs, provisions the unique ⁠VID-YYMM-XXXXX⁠ identifier, and displays the summary on-screen. Linking the external SD request numbers during creation ensures seamless cross-system tracking between Fin Core and SCORE.
3.3 Defining Voucher & Issue Categories
When configuring a new VID, administrators must define the specific classifications for the transaction.
￼ Voucher Category: Select a predefined category to classify the voucher request. To define a custom classification, choose the 'Other' option. This allows you to establish specific validation rules and parameter restrictions.
￼ Issue Category (Granular Classification): A multi-select tool where administrators pinpoint the exact systemic or operational issues triggering the voucher. It includes an "Add Issue" fail-safe option if the specific operational problem is not already listed in the master records.
3.4 Description and Allowed Roles
This step finalizes the context and security layer of the VID creation process.
￼ Description: A mandatory text field (up to 500 characters) where the creator must provide the detailed analysis, justification, or business reason for the voucher posting.
￼ Allowed Target Roles: A mandatory security dropdown. The creator must define exactly which system role is permitted to use this VID. If a role is not selected here, users in that role will not even see the VID as an option when attempting to create a batch.